;; Calling a suspending trait method from an ordinary function.
;;
;; A dispatched method arrives as a node of its own rather than as an
;; application, so there is no arrow for the usual yield-point test to read. The
;; colour rides on the node instead, put there when the call was lowered, from
;; the trait's declaration — the same choice a foreign call makes with its
;; `#:async` metadata, and for the same reason: by the time this is asked, the
;; registry that knew is several passes behind.
;;
;; Without it the program was accepted here and rejected by Roslyn, with
;; "the 'await' operator can only be used within an async method" against a
;; method the reader did not write.
;;
;; The receiver is generic, so this call goes through a dictionary. Its twin
;; below is the concrete one, which resolves statically — the two must be
;; refused alike.

;; EXPECT-ERROR: calling 'fetch' is a yield point
;; EXPECT-ERROR: the trait that declares it writes -bjo->
;; EXPECT-ERROR: is defined with (defun ...)

(import (std prelude))

(def/trait (Fetcher %a)
  (: fetch (-bjo-> %a string)))

(impl (Fetcher int)
  (defbjo (fetch x) (int->string x)))

(: bad (-> %a string) (where (Fetcher %a)))
(defun (bad x) (fetch x))

(defun (main args) 0)
